\section{Applications to symmetric cubic graphs}\label{s2}

Here are some applications of Theorem~\ref{t1} and Definitions~\ref{d1} and~\ref{d2} to symmetric cubic graphs, meaning graphs that are both vertex-transitive and edge-transitive.
Cases of cubic hamiltonian graphs may use the LCF notation for their expression \cite{CFP,Frucht}. In the cases of distance transitive graphs, their intersection arrays \cite{brower} will be mentioned, too.

